\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Layer merging in the Whisper decoder: each student layer is a weighted average of two consecutive teacher layers, then trained with cross-entropy and knowledge distillation.}}{60}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Head shearing of the Whisper embedding through low-rank decomposition. The decomposition is activation-aware because the low-rank weights are subsequently feature-distilled.}}{62}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Choice of \(\alpha \) and \(\beta \). Bayesian hyperparameter search indicates that the main constraint is to keep the lower-layer weight \(\alpha \) small.}}{65}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Activation similarities between all decoder-layer pairs. Consecutive layers tend to be more similar, suggesting that they can be merged.}}{66}{figure.caption.71}%
